## **WRITING GUIDELINES**

- **Metadata Block**: 6-8 lines, includes COMMAND, CATEGORY, AUTOFIX, SCOPE, DEPENDENCIES, PORTABILITY
- **Purpose**: 10-15 lines, one sentence description + When to Use + Expected Duration
- **Usage**: 15-25 lines, basic usage + 3-5 examples
- **Parameters**: 5-15 lines, table format with defaults, optional enum details
- **What It Does**: 10-20 lines, description + Important notes + Standards Applied + Available Fixes
- **Workflow**: 6-12 lines, enumerate Scan → Report → Prompt → Apply (note when prompts apply)
- **Integration**: 10-20 lines, Makefile example, optional CI/CD
- **Related Commands**: 2-5 lines, links to related commands
- **Footer**: 4 lines, Last Updated, Compatibility, Model Tested, Exit Codes

## **QUALITY TARGETS**

- **80-100 lines**: Simple, focused commands (e.g., `/test`)
- **100-120 lines**: Standard commands with parameters and integration (e.g., `00-docs-check`)
- **120-130 lines**: Complex commands with multiple parameters and extensive integration

## **COMMAND QUALITY CHECKLIST**

### **Structure & Length**

- [ ] Length within 80-130 line target range
- [ ] Metadata block includes all required fields (COMMAND, CATEGORY, AUTOFIX, SCOPE, DEPENDENCIES, PORTABILITY)
- [ ] Purpose section includes When to Use and Expected Duration
- [ ] Usage section has basic usage + 3-5 examples
- [ ] Parameters use table format with defaults
- [ ] What It Does includes Important notes about never auto-applying fixes
- [ ] Workflow section documents Scan → Report → Prompt → Apply (and when prompts occur)
- [ ] Integration includes Makefile example
- [ ] Footer includes Last Updated, Compatibility, Model Tested, Exit Codes

### **Consistency & Standards**

- [ ] Follows template exactly for predictable AI processing
- [ ] Consistent structure with existing commands
- [ ] Command name follows naming convention (`00-*` for universal commands, `/test` for special commands)
- [ ] Universal design - works on any project type
- [ ] References rules for validation criteria (doesn't duplicate rule content)

### **AI Optimization**

- [ ] Focuses on "how to use" not "what to check" (that's in rules)
- [ ] Actionable usage guidance over theoretical concepts
- [ ] Logical flow from purpose → usage → parameters → integration
- [ ] Clear examples showing real usage patterns
- [ ] Token-efficient content (no redundancy with rules)

### **Safety & User Experience**

- [ ] Explicitly states "Never auto-applies fixes" in What It Does
- [ ] Explicitly states "Never generates summary/md files" in What It Does
- [ ] All fix capabilities require explicit flags and user confirmation
- [ ] Clear exit codes documented
- [ ] Integration examples are copy-pasteable

**CRITICAL**: Commands focus on HOW to use. Rules contain WHAT to check. Commands reference rules instead of duplicating detailed validation criteria!
